        <div class="stat-item">
          <span class="stat-lbl" id="s4-lbl">Scale</span>
          <span class="stat-val" id="s4-val">Boiling</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="calc-right">
    <div class="card">
      <h2>Celsius to Fahrenheit Formula</h2>
      <p>To convert Celsius to Fahrenheit, multiply by 9/5 (1.8) and add 32. To convert back, subtract 32 and multiply by 5/9 (0.5556).</p>
      <div class="formula-box">°F = (°C × 1.8) + 32 &nbsp;|&nbsp; °C = (°F − 32) × 0.5556</div>
      <div class="answer-box">
        <div class="answer-row"><span class="answer-lbl">Freezing point</span><span class="answer-val">0°C = 32°F</span></div>
        <div class="answer-row"><span class="answer-lbl">Body temperature</span><span class="answer-val">37°C = 98.6°F</span></div>
        <div class="answer-row"><span class="answer-lbl">Room temperature</span><span class="answer-val">20°C = 68°F</span></div>
        <div class="answer-row"><span class="answer-lbl">Boiling point</span><span class="answer-val">100°C = 212°F</span></div>
        <div class="answer-row"><span class="answer-lbl">Same on both scales</span><span class="answer-val">−40° = −40°</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h2>Temperature Conversion Chart</h2>
      <p>Click any row to load it into the converter above.</p>
      <div style="overflow-x:auto">
      <table class="ref-tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Celsius (°C)</th><th>Fahrenheit (°F)</th><th>Reference</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {[
            [-40,'-40','Same on both scales'],
            [-20,'-4','Cold winter day'],
            [-10,'14','Below freezing'],
            [0,'32','Water freezes'],
            [10,'50','Cool day'],
            [20,'68','Room temperature'],
            [25,'77','Warm day'],
            [30,'86','Hot summer day'],
            [37,'98.6','Body temperature'],
            [40,'104','Fever / very hot day'],
            [100,'212','Water boils'],
            [180,'356','Oven — slow bake'],
            [200,'392','Oven — moderate'],
          ].map(([c, f, ref]) => (
            <tr onclick={`setVal(${c})`} class={c === 37 ? 'hi' : ''}>
              <td>{c}°C</td><td class="acc">{f}°F</td><td>{ref}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h2>How to Convert Celsius to Fahrenheit</h2>
      <p>The Celsius scale sets 0° as the freezing point and 100° as the boiling point of water. The Fahrenheit scale sets 32° as freezing and 212° as boiling. To go between them, use the formula: <strong>°F = (°C × 1.8) + 32</strong>.</p>
      <div class="tip-box">
        <span class="tip-icon">🌡️</span>
        <p><strong>Quick estimate:</strong> Double the °C value and add 30 to get an approximate °F. For 20°C: (20 × 2) + 30 = 70°F (exact: 68°F). Accurate within a few degrees for everyday temperatures.</p>
      </div>
      <h3>Oven temperature conversions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>150°C</strong> = 302°F (low/slow oven)</li>
        <li><strong>175°C</strong> = 347°F (moderate oven — most baking)</li>
        <li><strong>200°C</strong> = 392°F (moderately hot oven)</li>
        <li><strong>220°C</strong> = 428°F (hot oven — pizza, bread)</li>
        <li><strong>250°C</strong> = 482°F (very hot oven)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Why Temperature Conversion Matters</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>International recipes</strong> — European and UK recipes list oven temperatures in °C; US recipes use °F. Incorrect conversion leads to over- or under-baked food.</li>
        <li><strong>Weather forecasts</strong> — weather apps from different countries report temperature in different units. Convert to understand conditions when traveling.</li>
        <li><strong>Medical & health</strong> — body temperature 37°C (98.6°F) is normal; fever typically begins at 38°C (100.4°F). Thermometers in different countries use different scales.</li>
        <li><strong>Science & chemistry</strong> — most scientific work uses Celsius or Kelvin. US lab equipment often displays in Fahrenheit — convert to maintain consistent data.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>How do I convert Celsius to Fahrenheit?</h3>
        <p>Use the formula: <strong>°F = (°C × 1.8) + 32</strong>. Example: 25°C × 1.8 + 32 = 77°F.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What is 37°C in Fahrenheit?</h3>
        <p>37°C = <strong>98.6°F</strong>. This is normal human body temperature. Formula: 37 × 1.8 + 32 = 98.6°F.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What is 100°C in Fahrenheit?</h3>
        <p>100°C = <strong>212°F</strong>. This is the boiling point of water at sea level (1 atm pressure).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What is 0°C in Fahrenheit?</h3>
        <p>0°C = <strong>32°F</strong>. This is the freezing point of water. Below this temperature, water becomes ice.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What temperature is the same in °C and °F?</h3>
        <p><strong>−40°</strong> is the same in both Celsius and Fahrenheit. This is the only temperature where both scales agree.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>How do I convert Fahrenheit to Celsius?</h3>
        <p>Use: <strong>°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9</strong>. Example: (98.6 − 32) × 5/9 = 37°C. Use the °F → °C mode above.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What is room temperature in Fahrenheit?</h3>
        <p>Room temperature is typically 20–22°C = <strong>68–72°F</strong>. The most commonly used "standard" room temperature is 20°C (68°F).</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Related Converters</h2>
      <div class="next-grid">
        <a href="/kg-to-lbs" class="next-card">
